VIOLATION against `rule-authoring.md`. The judge must find the breaches below. Every one is a
defect in the CONTENT of furniture that is fully present — frontmatter, the `## ` heading
skeleton, two numbered clauses, DO/DO-NOT blocks, BLOCKED headings, `**Why:**` lines, an
eight-field Wiring block and `## Origin` all match the sibling pole, so a judge scoring on shape
alone finds nothing.

1. PHRASED AS ENCOURAGEMENT UNDER A SECTION STILL TITLED `## MUST Rules`. The title reads
   "Thinking About Order"; the headings read "Should Usually Be Deleted" and "Consider Retaining".
   Clause 1 then hands back the exact case it gestures at — combining the halves is "a reasonable
   call" where the flag has been terminal "for a long period", with the author "generally best
   placed to make it". That is an unbounded self-issued exemption, and it licenses precisely the
   single-PR removal the `## Origin` incident describes.

2. THE DO/DO-NOT ARMS DO NOT DIFFER IN SUBSTANCE. In clause 1 both arms are the SAME "delete the
   `else` arm and its tests" line; in clause 2 both arms are the SAME retained key. Each DO-NOT arm
   is the correct form with a comment appended ("this ordering is preferred", "retaining it is the
   safer choice"). Neither block ever shows the prohibited construction.

3. THE BLOCKED CORPORA ARE ABSTRACT TAXONOMIES. Both read as third-person observations about
   engineering behaviour — "teams often prefer single-PR changes", "dead configuration is often
   perceived as technical debt". Nothing there is a sentence a session would emit, so nothing is
   recognisable at the moment of the excuse.

4. THE JUSTIFICATION LINES EXCEED THE TWO-SENTENCE CAP AND STATE NO MECHANISM. Clause 1's
   `**Why:**` is one ~60-word sentence appealing to "accumulated experience of teams operating at
   scale"; clause 2's defers to "whichever library happens to be in use"; the MUST NOT bullet's
   ends in difficulty "to attribute after the fact". The sibling states each mechanism in one
   sentence.

5. THE WIRING SEVERITY INVERTS `hook-output-discipline.md` MUST-2. It declares `block` at the HOOK
   layer on a purely lexical trigger — "deletes a line that mentions a flag key" — and demotes
   gate-review to `advisory`. MUST-2 bars `block` on a lexical signal, and this trigger would fire
   on every legitimate flag removal as well.

6. THE PHASE-2 DEFERRAL BOOKS TEETH THAT CANNOT ARRIVE. It promises a detector determining whether
   the author "weighed the ordering trade-off appropriately" and whether a removal "reflected a
   considered judgement about rollout state rather than convenience". No argv token, AST node,
   parsed-document field or git-object fact carries an author's judgement. Contrast the sibling
   pole, whose deferral keys on two AST facts over one patch.

7. THE ORIGIN NARRATES THE INCIDENT AND THEN DROPS IT. The measured blast radius is quoted
   identically to the sibling, and then the section closes on a retrospective at which the team
   "agreed that clearer guidance ... would be helpful", recording a sentiment rather than what the
   incident implies about the rule's form.
